"""Phase 2.1 conversation batch C: everyday advice, explanations and opinions in many registers."""
from gen21_common import *

B = Bank("general_conversation", "c21c")
d = B.d

d("everyday", "what should i do with a free saturday, im in a small town", "A few ideas: a long walk or bike ride to somewhere you haven't been, a farmers market or local cafe, a library visit to pick up something unexpected, or a small project like cooking something new. Want something active, social or quiet?")
d("everyday", "my houseplant has yellow leaves", "Yellow leaves usually mean overwatering, but can also be too little light or a need for feeding. Check the soil: if it's wet a few centimetres down, water less often and make sure the pot drains. Remove the yellow leaves so the plant can focus on healthy growth.")
d("everyday", "ways to be less on my phone?", "Move distracting apps off the home screen, turn off non-essential notifications, set app time limits, and charge the phone outside the bedroom. Replace the habit with something specific, like a book at the spot where you usually scroll.")
d("everyday", "How do I stop my glasses fogging with a mask?", "Make sure the top of the mask is tight on your nose, with the glasses sitting over it. A folded tissue under the top edge helps, and so does washing the lenses with soapy water and letting them air dry, which leaves a thin film that reduces fog.")
d("everyday", "any tips for a first apartment?", "Budget for the extras: deposit, utilities, internet and basic furniture. Start with essentials: bed, a few pots and pans, cleaning supplies and a toolkit. Read the lease for repair rules, take dated photos of the condition when you move in, and learn where your water shutoff and fuse box are.")
d("everyday", "what do I say at a funeral", "Keep it simple and sincere: \"I'm so sorry for your loss,\" or \"They meant a lot to me.\" A short memory of the person is welcome if you have one. You don't have to say anything profound, and your presence matters more than words.")
d("everyday", "Is it normal to feel lonely after moving cities?", "Yes, very. Rebuilding a social life takes months. Join something that meets regularly, like a class, club or sports team, since repeated contact is what turns acquaintances into friends. Keep in touch with old friends too, and give yourself some time.")
d("everyday", "quick tip to remember names?", "Repeat the name right away in conversation (\"Nice to meet you, Priya\"), link it to a feature or a person you know with the same name, and use it once or twice more before the conversation ends.")
d("everyday", "how do you politely decline an invite", "\"Thank you so much for inviting me. I won't be able to make it, but I hope it goes wonderfully.\" You don't need to give a detailed reason. If you'd like to meet another time, add, \"Can we catch up next week?\"")
d("everyday", "i feel guilty saying no to people", "Many people do. A no isn't a rejection of the person, only of the request. Try a short, kind version: \"I can't take that on right now.\" You can offer a smaller alternative if you want, but you don't have to, and over time it feels easier.")
d("everyday", "Best way to learn a language fast?", "Daily contact beats long weekly sessions. Combine listening (podcasts, shows), speaking with a tutor or exchange partner, and spaced-repetition for vocabulary. Learn the 1000 most common words first, and speak from week one even if you make mistakes.")
d("everyday", "How can I save money on groceries?", "Plan meals and make a list, check what you already have, buy staples in bulk, compare unit prices, choose store brands, and cook larger batches to freeze. Frozen vegetables are good value and reduce waste.")
d("everyday", "I never know what to say on dates", "Prepare a few open questions you're curious about (\"What's something you've been into lately?\") and follow up on answers instead of moving to the next question. Sharing small stories of your own keeps it balanced, and silences are fine.")
d("everyday", "should i wake up early to be productive", "Not necessarily. Productivity depends more on sleep and focus than on the clock. If you're naturally an early riser, mornings can be great, but forcing a schedule against your rhythm usually backfires. Aim for consistent sleep and put your hardest task when you feel sharpest.")
d("everyday", "how do I learn to cook with no experience", "Start with 5 to 10 simple dishes you'd like to eat: pasta with sauce, scrambled eggs, a stir-fry, roast chicken, a soup. Follow recipes exactly the first time, learn basic knife skills, and taste as you go. Repeating the same dishes builds confidence faster than constantly trying new ones.")
d("everyday", "my neighbors are loud at night, what do I do", "Start with a friendly, specific conversation: they may not realise how far sound carries. If that doesn't work, a polite note, then your landlord or building manager, and local noise rules if it continues. Keep a short log of dates and times in case you need it.")
d("curious", "why do onions make you cry", "Cutting an onion breaks cells and releases compounds that react to form a gas, syn-propanethial-S-oxide, which irritates your eyes. Your eyes respond with tears. A sharp knife, a chilled onion or cutting near an extractor fan can reduce it.")
d("curious", "why is the ocean salty", "Rivers carry dissolved minerals from rocks into the sea. Water evaporates, but the salt stays behind, and over millions of years it has built up. Volcanic vents on the seafloor add some too.")
d("curious", "what's a black hole actually", "A region where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape once it crosses the boundary called the event horizon. Many form when massive stars collapse at the end of their lives.")
d("curious", "Why do we dream?", "Scientists don't fully agree. Leading ideas are that dreaming helps process emotions and memories, plays a role in learning, or is a by-product of brain activity during REM sleep. We do know vivid dreams are most common in REM sleep.")
d("curious", "how do magnets work?", "Magnetism comes from moving electric charge. In a magnet, the spins of many electrons line up, creating a magnetic field around the material. Opposite poles attract and like poles repel.")
d("curious", "why do we get hiccups", "A hiccup is a sudden spasm of the diaphragm followed by the vocal cords snapping shut, which makes the sound. Common triggers are eating fast, fizzy drinks or excitement. They usually stop on their own in minutes.")
d("curious", "Where does the word 'salary' come from?", "From the Latin 'salarium', linked to 'sal', salt. It's often said Roman soldiers were paid in salt, although historians doubt that; it may have been money to buy salt, or the word may have another origin.")
d("curious", "do fish sleep?", "Yes, though differently. Most fish rest, slowing activity and reducing responsiveness, but they don't have eyelids and usually don't close their eyes. Some sharks must keep swimming to breathe, but they still have rest periods.")
d("curious", "how are rainbows formed", "Sunlight enters raindrops, bends, reflects off the back of the drop and bends again leaving. Different colours bend by different amounts, so the light spreads into a spectrum. You see a rainbow when the sun is behind you and rain is in front.")
d("curious", "why is yawning contagious?", "It's not fully understood. Ideas include empathy and social bonding, since people yawn more in response to those they're close to, and an old mirroring reflex. It doesn't seem to be only about tiredness.")
d("curious", "how big is the universe", "The observable universe is about 93 billion light-years across. Beyond that, we don't know: it may be much larger or even infinite, but we can't see past the edge of the observable part.")
d("curious", "what is the difference between a crocodile and an alligator", "Alligators have broad, U-shaped snouts and their lower teeth are hidden when the mouth is closed. Crocodiles have narrower, V-shaped snouts, and the fourth lower tooth is visible on the outside. Alligators prefer fresh water; some crocodiles tolerate salt water.")
d("curious", "Is it true we swallow spiders in our sleep", "It's a myth. Sleeping people make noise and vibrations that would put a spider off, and there's no real evidence of anyone regularly swallowing them.")
d("opinion", "What do you think about remote work?", "It depends on the person and the job. Remote work offers flexibility, no commute and quiet focus, but can hurt spontaneous collaboration and make it harder for newer staff to learn. Hybrid arrangements try to get the best of both. What's your situation?")
d("opinion", "do you think social media is bad for teens?", "The evidence is mixed. Heavy use is associated with worse mood and sleep for some teens, particularly around comparison and bullying, but causation is hard to prove and many teens also benefit from connection. Limits on late-night use and conversations about what they see tend to help.")
d("opinion", "Pineapple on pizza: yes or no?", "It's a matter of taste. The sweet and salty contrast with ham works for many people, and the tang cuts through the cheese. If you like it, nothing is wrong with that.")
d("opinion", "is college worth it", "For many, but it depends on the field, cost and alternatives. A degree often raises lifetime earnings and is required in some careers, but debt can outweigh the gain if the course has weak job prospects. Trades, apprenticeships and certificates can also be strong paths.")
d("opinion", "which is better, cats or dogs", "Depends on what you want. Dogs offer companionship and activity, but need walks and time. Cats are more independent and suit smaller spaces, but still need attention. Think about your schedule, allergies and living space.")
d("opinion", "Do you think I should text my ex?", "I can't know the details, but ask yourself what you want from it. If it's closure or to say something useful, that may be fine. If it's loneliness or hope of getting back together, waiting a day often helps. What's making you consider it?")
d("opinion", "morning shower or evening shower?", "Either works. Mornings help you wake up; evenings rinse off the day and can help you wind down, and keep bedding cleaner. If you have dry skin, a shorter lukewarm shower helps either way.")
d("opinion", "is it weird that I like being alone", "No. Enjoying solitude is healthy and common. It's a concern only if you feel isolated or lonely, or you avoid people out of anxiety. If you feel good and have people you like when you want them, it's just a preference.")
d("advice_short", "How do I focus better?", "Work in 25-minute blocks with short breaks, remove your phone from sight, and write down one clear task before you begin. Sleep and movement help more than most tricks.")
d("advice_short", "any advice for a shy person at a party?", "Arrive early when it's quieter, ask people questions about themselves, and find one other person who also seems uneasy. You can set a time limit to feel safe: \"I'll stay an hour.\"")
d("advice_short", "How do I get over a breakup?", "Give yourself time to grieve, cut down contact for a while, lean on friends, keep your routines (sleep, food, movement), and avoid checking their social media. It tends to hurt less each week, even if it doesn't feel that way yet.")
d("advice_short", "tips for public speaking?", "Know your opening and ending, rehearse aloud, pause often, and speak a bit slower than feels natural. Focus on helping the audience, not on being perfect.")
d("advice_short", "how do I start journaling", "Write for five minutes a day with a simple prompt, like \"What happened today and how did it feel?\" You don't need to write well. Keep the notebook where you'll see it.")
d("advice_short", "Best way to apologise?", "Name what you did, say sorry without \"but\", acknowledge the effect on the other person, and say what you'll do differently. Then give them space instead of asking for immediate forgiveness.")
d("advice_short", "how do I stop overthinking", "Write the worry down, then ask: is there something I can do about it today? If yes, plan one step. If no, redirect with something absorbing like a walk or a call. Setting a ten-minute \"worry time\" can stop it taking over your day.")
d("advice_short", "tips for a long flight", "Pick an aisle or window based on your habits, drink water, walk every hour or two, wear layers and bring headphones, a charger and a snack. Setting your watch to the destination time early helps with jet lag.")
d("advice_short", "How do I make friends as an adult?", "Go where the same people meet regularly: classes, sports clubs, volunteering, board game nights. Take the initiative to follow up with a specific plan: \"Want to grab coffee after class Thursday?\" Friendships often take about 50 hours together to start.")
d("advice_short", "what is the best way to wake up on time", "Set the alarm across the room, keep the same wake time every day, get daylight soon after, and go to bed at a consistent hour. Don't rely on snooze; it leaves you more groggy.")
d("confusion_help", "I'm confused about the difference between a stock and a bond", "A stock is a share of ownership in a company: you gain if the company grows and may receive dividends, but you can lose money. A bond is a loan to a company or government: they pay you regular interest and return the principal at maturity. Bonds are generally steadier, stocks generally riskier with higher potential returns.")
d("confusion_help", "What's the difference between a Roth and a traditional IRA?", "In a traditional IRA, you may deduct contributions now and pay tax when you withdraw in retirement. In a Roth, you pay tax now and qualified withdrawals are tax-free. Roth often suits people who expect a higher tax rate later; income limits and rules apply, and rules change, so check current figures.")
d("confusion_help", "What does 'per annum' mean", "It means per year. If a loan charges 6% per annum, that's 6% each year.")
d("confusion_help", "what's the difference between sympathy and empathy", "Sympathy is feeling sorry for someone: \"I'm sorry that's happening.\" Empathy is trying to feel or understand what they feel: \"That sounds so hard; I'd be anxious too.\" Both help, but empathy usually connects more.")
d("confusion_help", "difference between 'its' and 'it's'?", "\"It's\" is short for \"it is\" or \"it has\". \"Its\" shows possession: \"The dog wagged its tail.\" Test: replace with \"it is\"; if the sentence still works, use \"it's\".")
d("confusion_help", "What is a mortgage in plain english?", "A mortgage is a loan to buy property, secured by the property. You repay it in monthly instalments with interest over many years. If you stop paying, the lender can take the property.")
d("confusion_help", "difference between a virus and malware", "Malware is the umbrella term for any harmful software. A computer virus is one type of malware that spreads by attaching to other files or programs. Others include ransomware, spyware and trojans.")
d("confusion_help", "what's a 'light-year'? is it time?", "No, it's a distance: how far light travels in one year, about 9.46 trillion kilometres. The name is confusing because 'year' is a time unit.")
d("confusion_help", "Is a tomato a fruit or a vegetable?", "Botanically it's a fruit, since it develops from the flower's ovary and contains seeds. In cooking it's treated as a vegetable because it is savoury.")
d("confusion_help", "what's the difference between 'sue' and 'prosecute'", "To sue is a civil action: one person or company brings a case against another, usually seeking compensation. To prosecute is a criminal action: the state brings charges for a crime. A single event can lead to both.")
d("confusion_help", "Why do some words have silent letters like knife?", "English spelling kept older pronunciations. 'Knife' once had a spoken 'k', but speakers dropped the sound while the spelling stayed the same. Many silent letters are fossils of how words used to be said or of their French and Latin origins.")
d("frustrated_user", "this app keeps logging me out!!!", "That is annoying. Common causes are blocked cookies, an expired session, or the app clearing data. Which app is it, and is it on the web or on your phone? That'll let me suggest something specific.")
d("frustrated_user", "I've been on hold with customer service for an hour", "That's exhausting. If you stay on, keep a note of the time and the representative's name. Many companies have a callback option, chat or social media support, which are sometimes faster. I can help draft a short message if you try that route.")
d("frustrated_user", "Why does nothing ever work the first time?", "It does feel that way when you're stuck. Often the first attempt reveals what you didn't know, which isn't failure but data. What's not working at the moment? I can help you narrow it down.")
d("frustrated_user", "my printer says offline but it's on", "Typical fixes: check the printer is on the same Wi-Fi as your computer, restart both, set it as the default printer and untick 'Use Printer Offline' in the print queue. If that fails, remove and re-add the printer or update its driver.")
d("beginner", "What is a spreadsheet used for?", "A spreadsheet organises data in rows and columns, and lets you calculate with formulas. People use them for budgets, schedules, lists, grades and simple analysis, such as totals and averages. Excel and Google Sheets are common examples.")
d("beginner", "what's a VPN", "A VPN (virtual private network) sends your internet traffic through an encrypted connection to another server. It hides your activity from your local network, such as public wifi, and masks your IP address from sites. It doesn't make you anonymous, and you have to trust the VPN provider.")
d("beginner", "what's the cloud", "'The cloud' means computers owned by a company and accessed over the internet. When you save a photo to cloud storage, it lives on their servers instead of just your device.")
d("beginner", "how does wifi work", "A router sends data as radio waves to your devices, which have antennas to receive them and send replies. The router is connected to the internet by cable and passes the data along.")
d("beginner", "what's a good password", "Long matters most: a passphrase of four or more unrelated words, or a random string from a password manager. Use a different password for each site, and turn on two-factor authentication where possible.")
d("beginner", "what is machine learning in simple terms", "Instead of writing rules by hand, you show a program many examples and it learns patterns that it can apply to new cases. For instance, showing it thousands of labelled photos teaches it to recognise cats.")
d("beginner", "what are cookies on websites", "Small files that a site stores in your browser to remember things like your login, preferences or items in a cart. Some are used for tracking across sites, which is why many sites ask for your consent.")
d("beginner", "how do I know if an email is a scam", "Warning signs: urgent threats, requests for passwords or payments, mismatched sender addresses, odd links (hover to check), and poor spelling. When unsure, go to the company's website directly instead of clicking the link.")
d("expert", "I'm a physics grad student. Can you explain why the Higgs field gives mass to particles, without hand-waving?", "In the Standard Model, the Higgs field has a non-zero value everywhere (vacuum expectation value v, about 246 GeV). Fermion masses come from Yukawa couplings: a term y times (left-handed fermion) times (Higgs) times (right-handed fermion) becomes a mass term m = y v / sqrt(2) once the field takes its value. The W and Z bosons get mass from the Higgs mechanism: their coupling to the Higgs doublet, with electroweak symmetry breaking, absorbs Goldstone modes as longitudinal polarisations. The Higgs boson is the excitation of the field about that value. Note that most of the mass of ordinary matter (protons, neutrons) comes from QCD binding energy, not the Higgs.")
d("expert", "I work in logistics. Why would I use a linear program instead of a heuristic?", "A linear program (LP) gives a provably optimal answer for linear objectives and constraints, and tells you through duals what each constraint costs you. Use it when your problem is naturally linear, such as flows, blending, allocation, and when solver time is acceptable. A heuristic is better when the problem is combinatorial and large, like vehicle routing with many constraints, or the model needs to be fast and approximate.")
d("expert", "I'm a lawyer. Briefly, what is 'promissory estoppel'?", "A doctrine that can enforce a promise without consideration when the promisee reasonably relied on it to their detriment and injustice can be avoided only by enforcement. It's used as a shield or sword, depending on the jurisdiction. Exact elements and remedies vary, so check your jurisdiction's authorities.")
d("expert", "Can you explain the CAP theorem to someone who knows distributed systems basics?", "In the presence of a network partition, a system must choose between consistency (every read sees the latest write) and availability (every request gets a response). Partitions are unavoidable, so the practical choice is CP or AP behaviour during partitions. Many systems tune this per operation, and PACELC extends it: even without partitions, there is a trade-off between latency and consistency.")
d("long_question", "So I'm 29, I've been working in marketing for 6 years, I make decent money but I feel like I'm not learning anything anymore and I'm kind of bored. I've thought about switching to data analytics because I like numbers and Excel, but I also worry that I'd be starting from scratch and taking a pay cut. My partner thinks I should stay put until we buy a house. How should I think about this?", "A useful way to frame it: this is less \"switch or stay\" and more \"how do I test the switch cheaply.\" Marketing experience is not starting from scratch; analysts who understand campaigns and customers are valued. You could spend 3 to 6 months on SQL, Excel/BI tools and a small project using real marketing data, perhaps move toward a marketing analytics role at your current company, and see if you enjoy it. That reduces the pay risk, and the house timeline can be part of the plan: you and your partner can decide how big a pay dip you could accept and for how long. If you tell me your rough numbers, we could work through that.")
d("long_question", "My landlord wants to raise rent by 12 percent starting next month, he only told me last week, I've been in the flat for 3 years with no issues and always paid on time, and honestly I can't afford it but I also don't want to move because I love my neighbourhood. what are my options", "First, check your local rules: many places require written notice with a minimum period and may cap increases, so find out whether 12% and one month's notice are allowed where you live. Then consider negotiating. A tenant who pays on time and stays long saves the landlord vacancy costs, so you can ask for a smaller increase or a phased one, ideally in writing. Compare similar rents nearby so you know if 12% is market. If it holds, explore a flatmate, or ask whether a longer lease could lock in a rate. Moving is your fallback, not your starting point. Tell me your country or city and I can be more specific on tenant rules.")
d("short_blunt", "pizza dough rest time", "At least 1 hour at room temperature for a quick dough; 24 to 72 hours in the fridge for better flavour.")
d("short_blunt", "convert 180 C to F", "356 F.")
d("short_blunt", "synonym for happy", "Cheerful, content, joyful, glad, pleased, delighted.")
d("short_blunt", "how many weeks in a year", "52 weeks, plus one or two extra days.")
d("short_blunt", "speed of light", "About 299,792 kilometres per second.")
d("short_blunt", "spell necessary", "N-e-c-e-s-s-a-r-y. One collar (c), two sleeves (s).")
d("short_blunt", "10 percent of 450", "45.")
d("short_blunt", "is 91 prime", "No, 91 = 7 x 13.")
d("short_blunt", "largest planet", "Jupiter.")
d("short_blunt", "who wrote pride and prejudice", "Jane Austen, published in 1813.")
